  "body": "A small, separate slice of the post-merge work. #2908 (lanes, claimed by the maintainer) stays as it is. Proposed in the mkinney comments on #2908 of 2026-11-21.\n\n## What happens now\n\n`post-merge.yml` uses one concurrency group for `main` (`post-merge-main`, `cancel-in-progress: false`). One run at a time; a second push waits, and a third push cancels the waiting one. A merge batch costs cancelled runs, and a red run covers several merges, so finding the breaking merge needs a bisect (#3111 took hours).\n\n## Wanted\n\n1. One concurrency group per commit: `post-merge-${{ inputs.ref || github.sha }}`, `cancel-in-progress: false`. Each push runs on its own free `camcraft-post-merge` runner and always finishes; nothing waits behind another run and nothing is cancelled. The `gate` job does not change. With today's two runners (wopr, uma) two merges are gated at once; more runners only add capacity.\n2. Results can now finish out of order. The `report` job gets its own concurrency group (`post-merge-report`, `cancel-in-progress: false`) and a full-history checkout. `.github/scripts/post-merge-report.sh` keeps the last reported commit in a hidden marker in the rolling issue body (`<!-- post-merge-last-sha: <sha> -->`) and skips (notice only) a run whose commit is an ancestor of that sha and not equal to it (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). No new secrets or repository variables.\n3. An xtask test (like `xtask/tests/post_merge_report.rs`) pins: the per-commit group, `cancel-in-progress: false`, and the report skipping an older commit.\n\nThis is a workflow change: the maintainer decides (the review labels it `needs-maintainer`).\n\nDone means: two pushes in a row each get a full post-merge run that finishes (neither is cancelled); a red run names exactly its own commit; an older green run never closes the rolling issue that a newer red run opened (pinned by the xtask test).\n",
